# Git-secrets patterns to detect
# One pattern per line - these will be added via: git config --add secrets.patterns
# Note: git-secrets uses grep, so we keep patterns simple and use .gitallowed for exceptions

# Private keys (exceptions handled in .gitallowed)
private[-_]key
api[-_]key
secret[-_]key
BEGIN (RSA|DSA|EC|OPENSSH) PRIVATE KEY

# AWS keys
AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16}

# NOTE: a generic high-entropy rule ([0-9a-zA-Z/+]{40}) used to live here and
# was removed deliberately.
#
# In a Nix repo, 40-character strings are structural rather than suspicious:
# commit SHAs, narHashes, pinned flake URLs. It fired on nearly every flake
# change, and .gitallowed had grown three separate exemptions to compensate.
# A check that cries wolf on routine work teaches you to reach for
# --no-verify, which is precisely when it would have caught something real.
#
# The remaining patterns are specific enough to mean something: they name the
# kind of credential rather than guessing from entropy. Real secrets here are
# sops-encrypted and committed as ciphertext by design; these rules are the
# backstop for a plaintext key pasted somewhere it should not be, which is a
# different failure and one the named patterns catch.
